# THE MANAGED / CUSTODIAL SUB-IDENTITY — measured findings

*Research 2026-09-04. **MEASURED** = date, number, or quoted primary text retrieved this session. **DESIGNED/CLAIMED** = vendor or spec intent. **NOT REACHED** = could not retrieve; not filled from memory.*

## HEADLINE ANSWERS

**Q1 (original): Has anyone shipped a managed identity the managed party can LEAVE with?**

**Essentially no one — and where the exit exists, single-digit percent use it.** Two systems make the answer measurable, and they agree on the order of magnitude:

- **Bluesky/atproto**, the closest live analogue: **~100,000 of 23,696,277 accounts (≈0.42%) have ever migrated off Bluesky's PDS fleet**; **97.66%** remain on Bluesky-operated infrastructure. Of 1,200 sampled bsky.social accounts, **1,196 (99.67%) hold *zero* rotation keys of their own** — control rests in two Bluesky PBC fleet keys. I independently verified this on three accounts including the CEO's (`jay.bsky.team`) and `bsky.app` itself: both keys are the company's. The documented exit path is `com.atproto.identity.requestPlcOperationSignature` — **the host signs the departure on your behalf, gated on an emailed code**. If you are suspended, the exit is gone with the email.
- **Farcaster**: `idCounter()` = **3,349,918** fids. In two random samples (n=298, n=300), **36–41% name the Warpcast Recovery Proxy** `0x00000000FcB080a4D6c39a9354dA9EB9bC104cd7`, which can transfer any of those identities to any address **instantly, with no consent and no timelock**. **~5%** name a recovery address of their own. The escape hatch is real, onchain, and almost nobody takes it.
- **Embedded wallets** (Privy, MetaMask/Web3Auth, Magic, Dynamic, Turnkey, Coinbase CDP): every one ships the same primitive — **a cross-origin iframe that reveals a raw private key**. That is a *fork of custody*, not a transfer: nothing is revoked, no relationship dissolves. **No provider has published any telemetry on export completion, anywhere reachable** (HN Algolia full corpus, Crossref, vendor docs — all empty). Structurally they cannot: the provider learns the modal opened, not that a key was kept. Two of six let the *application* switch export off entirely.
- **Enterprise: zero.** No production mechanism hands a corporate-provisioned identity to the person at exit. Managed Apple Accounts auto-delete 30d (manual) / 120d (SIS) after deactivation. Android work profiles are deleted wholesale. SCIM offers DELETE-or-hide and nothing else. The only org→person path found is Google's **"Transfer your content"** — a lossy **copy**, school accounts only, admin-gated, into an account the person already owned.

**Q2 (re-aimed): Does anything shipped have a management chain that never bottoms out in a person?**

**No. Nothing shipped is rootless.** Every system runs machine-to-machine chains of arbitrary depth at runtime and re-attaches a human at a layer the runtime cannot see.

| System | Runtime chain rootless? | Where the human re-attaches |
|---|---|---|
| Miller's ocap model | **Yes, by postulate** (§9.2.1 "first instant of time") | §11.3 *"the owner of the system… can run such code when the system is first generated"*; §7.4 the network root is literally *"the human operator… read the URI from his screen over the phone"* |
| Kubernetes | **Yes** — SAs create/empower SAs freely | Audit `Event` has exactly two slots, `User` + `ImpersonatedUser`, **both singular, neither required to be human, no ancestry field**. The human is in a *different* audit event: whoever applied the bootstrap RoleBinding |
| SPIFFE/SPIRE | **Yes** — self-signed root, nested servers attest servers | `spire-server entry create` at a shell; `admin_ids` in a config file; Delegated Identity requires *"allowed clients on the spire-agent configuration"* |
| GCP | Yes, N hops via the `delegates` field | Audit proto records the ordered chain and names them *"the real authorities"*; the payments profile carries *"the name, address, and tax ID… of who is responsible"* |
| AWS | Yes; `sourceIdentity` **off by default** | Customer Agreement §2.1 (Aug 14 2026): *"you must have an AWS account associated with a valid email address and a valid form of payment"*; `CreateAccount` requires an owner email that **becomes the root user** |
| Entra Agent ID | Service principals **may own** agent identities | **A sponsor is REQUIRED on create — users or groups only, never service principals — including "for app-only create requests"** |
| Ethereum | Safe-owns-Safe, arbitrary depth, no EOA required in the graph | *"Contract accounts… can only send messages in response to receiving a transaction."* The ownership graph is rootless; **it is also inert.** EIP-7702 (Final) does not change this |

**Custody is delegable to machines. Accountability is not.** Microsoft states it as policy; AWS states it as contract; Ethereum states it as physics.

---

## A. CHILDREN'S IDENTITY — the age transition

**Three invariants hold across Apple, Google, Microsoft (and Nintendo, checked as a fourth):**

1. **Nobody forks the identity.** The account is one durable object; age is a mutable attribute; the "transition" is a permission recomputation. Orphaning happens only through *deletion*, never through aging.
2. **The exit is gated on the custodian's cooperation, not the calendar.** Reaching the age of consent grants *standing to request*, never the ability to act.
3. **Entitlements are held by the group, not the person, and do not travel.**

**Apple** — https://support.apple.com/en-us/119854 (pub. 2026-07-01), verbatim: *"Under 13 — You need to be part of a Family Sharing group. Ages 13-17 — If your parent or guardian has set up Screen Time, you need permission from your family organizer to leave the group… Depending on the laws where you live, you might not be able to leave your Family Sharing group until you turn 18."*

The sharpest line, https://support.apple.com/en-us/102634: **"There's no other way to remove a child from a family group if they're under 13."** The two terminal states are *transfer to another custodian* or *destruction*. Compounding: *"The organizer can't leave the Family Sharing group"* — so an organizer with an under-13 child cannot exit at all.

Cost of leaving as an adult (https://support.apple.com/en-us/102652): you keep purchases you initiated, forfeit all shared/DRM content, and **"If you downloaded an app from a family member's purchase history and made in-app purchases, you'll need to purchase the app yourself to access your in-app purchases."** And: **"your balance transfers to the family organizer's Apple Cash account"** — a monetary asset moving *away* from the aging-out child at the moment of exit. Purchase consolidation is explicitly barred: *"Neither Apple Account can be a child account created through Family Sharing"* (117294). Correction to folklore: the limit is now **two** family groups per year, not one.

**2025 Declared Age Range API** (iOS/macOS 26.0) — the most interesting mechanism found. Verified verbatim: *"When a person's age crosses into a new range (for example, when they turn 13), the API continues returning the previous range until the anniversary of their original declaration."* A deliberate lag of up to 364 days, so apps cannot infer a birthday from the bucket flip. In regulated regions: *"the system automatically provides the person's age range — they can't decline sharing."*

**State app-store laws, MEASURED timeline** (Apple developer news): Texas SB2420 announced for 2026-01-01 → **injunction 2025-12-23** → **lifted, effective 2026-06-04**. Utah live for new accounts **2026-05-06**; Louisiana **2026-07-01**. Texas consumer page (https://support.apple.com/en-us/127462): *"If you're under 18, you need to be part of a Family Sharing group… Ask to Buy and some Screen Time features are automatically turned on if you're under 18 — and **can't be turned off — even by parents or guardians**… For all accounts, your age range is always shared with developers and can't be turned off."* Note: the state overrides the *guardian*, not just the child.

**Google Family Link** — a **measured correction to widely-cited behavior**. https://support.google.com/families/answer/7106787 now says three separate times that the release age is **18**, not 13: *"Children under 18 need parent approval to stop supervision."* The 13th birthday is an *email*; the default is inertia (*"by not acting on the email"*). Transfer of supervision: **1 switch per 12 months**, 7-day notify + 7-day accept. (I could not independently re-verify this page at end of session — Google rate-limited our egress with a CAPTCHA interstitial; the verbatim quotes are from a successful fetch earlier in the session.)

**Microsoft** — **there is no documented age transition at all.** The complete `support.microsoft.com/en-us/family-safety/` hub (19 articles) was enumerated; no article describes what happens at 13, 18, or any age. This is a genuine negative, not a search failure. Instead: *"Can a child edit their own Microsoft account date of birth? **No.**"* And only an organizer can remove anyone — **a plain member cannot leave, ever, by design.** Microsoft's own documented remedy for an adult mis-flagged as a child: *"Sign in with any adult personal Microsoft account **or create a new dummy adult account**."* That is the fix and the proof the gate is unenforceable, on a first-party page.

**Microsoft's admitted failure mode** — an entire support article, *"Family safety settings enforced on adults"*: *"**only the family organizer can make changes to your safety settings**. Please reach out to them…"* plus a per-device `schtasks /run /tn "…FamilySafetyRefreshTask"` for restrictions that persist after the group relationship is gone. **Supervision outliving the authority that justified it, vendor-acknowledged.**

**Two shipped platforms disagree about the legal age of digital majority in the same jurisdiction.** Chile: Google 14, Microsoft 18. Bulgaria: Google 16, Microsoft 14. Same child, two different account lifecycles.

**Complaints (Apple Communities; index appears frozen ~June 2022, so absence of recent threads is an artifact):** the recurring class is the under-13 immovability hit from every side. The sharpest: *"My child under 13 has passed away and I can not remove from family sharing"* (https://discussions.apple.com/thread/253959635) — the only answer in-thread is *call Apple Support*. **Death is not a case the mechanism models.** Also estrangement (*"don't have contact with the parent any longer"*), remarriage (organizer-can't-leave + under-13 composing into a total lock), and the wrong-birthday trap: HN 8409702 (2014-10-04) states the origin — *"they didn't come up with the way to convert any existing ids to child ids"* — and Apple's answer (the 14-day revert window, article 124146) **shipped in 2026, twelve years later.**

**NOT REACHED:** Reddit (403 to all forms), Google Help Communities (302), any Google/Microsoft user-side complaint evidence, Utah SB 142 primary text, Apple's Feb-2025 newsroom PDF.

