## B. GUARDIANSHIP IN SSI — the model, and its collapse

**Date correction to your brief:** the whitepaper you cited as "V2 ~2020" is **V.2, April 2023** (V1 was December 2019). https://sovrin.org/wp-content/uploads/Guardianship-Whitepaper-V2.0.pdf

**The model.** Guardian holds; Dependent is the subject. Trust-over-IP four-layer stack; W3C VCs whose schemas encode a jurisdiction's rights and duties. Sovrin Governance Framework principle: *"Guardians must respect the fundamental right of a dependent to reclaim self-sovereign identity if changes in the dependent's circumstances enable it."*

**The transition out — and this is the finding.** Stage 4, verbatim: *"As Mya achieves the age of maturity, she would look to be issued and manage her own credentials in the same way as Zo does as the guardian. **Mya is issued equivalent credentials for her own use.** Zo's credentials may not be actively revoked, but can be considered to be **practically expired**."* And for Jamie: *"Any newly added credentials issued to Ann… would have to be revoked and **Jamie to be issued his own replacement credentials**."*

**The identity does not transfer. It is re-issued.** The dependent gets *new* credentials from issuers who must still exist and still be willing. That is the same shape as Google's "Transfer your content": a copy into a fresh container, not a handover.

**Guardian abuse is named clearly, in three risk families.** *"Ironically, guardianship does precisely the opposite [of SSI]."* Named risks: **Recentralization** (mitigation: *"Prohibiting 'bulk load' processes where whole populations are converted to guardianship without involvement or consent"*); **Violating the trust relationship** (self-payment, fraud, refusing a requested transaction); **Impersonation and commingling** — with the paper's weakest claim: *"it is difficult for a guardian to misuse a verifiable credential because although they are the credential holder, they are not its subject."* And **Risks at moments of transition**: *"ensure that a dependent is not 'digitally stranded' with no guardian after a point of transition."*

**The wear-my-identity case is explicitly ruled against.** The Technical Requirements V1 (April 2021, retrieved from the Drive links in the launch blog post) lists **"Wallet Take-Over"** as *future work*: *"The ability to access another person's wallet, access its current credentials and use them (known as Wallet Take-Over as well as holder impersonation) may be called for in specific situations, **it should only be considered an option when everything else fails, because issuers nor verifiers can distinguish between the situation where the holder is, or is not, impersonated**."* Five years after that was written, it is still future work.

**Did any of it ship? No.**

- **`gh search code "guardianship" --owner hyperledger` → zero hits.** `gh search repos "guardianship SSI verifiable"` → zero. Global `GuardianshipCredential` search returns only architecture markdown, TODO lists, and glossaries — no production code.
- **Aries RFC 0289** (the ToIP stack RFC the whitepaper cites as its technical anchor): **Status STALLED since 2024-04-03**, status note *"No recent progress and **no implementations have been created**."* Implementations section empty.
- OpenWallet Foundation `architecture-sig#36` "Power of Attorney / Mandating / Guardianship": open since 2022-10-19, **2 comments, both on day one, untouched since**.
- The V2 paper asserts *"Deployment of Guardianship credentials for real world applications is happening"* — **with no footnote, and no named deployment anywhere in the document.**
- The whitepaper's footnotes 22/23 cite the Implementation Guidelines and Technical Requirements **without URLs**; neither is on sovrin.org's library, and Wayback shows only whitepaper PDFs ever archived from that host. The Guardianship WG Confluence wiki 404s. Archived WG meeting pages span **2020-12-11 to 2021-06-25** (Wayback coverage caveat).

**The named pilots are all dead:**
- **Kiva Protocol**: banner on https://www.kiva.org/protocol — *"Kiva will sunset operations of Kiva Protocol, **effective June 30, 2022**."*
- **ID2020**: absorbed into the Digital Impact Alliance, **August 2023**; site frozen at ©2023.
- **Tykn**: `tykn.tech` now **302s to a games site**. Domain resold.
- **Sovrin Foundation itself**: *"officially dissolved by the State of Utah on **May 21, 2025**"* after *"an extended period of reduced activity"* (announced 2026-01-20). MainNet is now **read-only, on a single virtual cloud server**, with **Trinsic as sole steward**, and *"Trinsic and the previous Trustees continue to hold MainNet Trustee keys."*
- Capstone: **Trinsic** — the last operator of the SSI guardianship ledger — now sells the opposite product. Its homepage: *"Accept the World's Digital IDs Through One API… mobile driver's licences, eIDAS, bank-based IDs, national ID wallets. 56 ID providers integrated."*

**Is there a normative spec? No.**
- **W3C VC Data Model 2.0, REC 15 May 2025**: `"guardian"` appears **0 times**; `"custodi"` 0; `"delegat"` once.
- **W3C DID Core 1.0, REC 19 July 2022**: `"guardian"` **0 times**. `capabilityDelegation` exists but is key authorization, not person guardianship.
- `w3c/vc-data-model#930` "Representing Delegated Credentials in the VCDM" — opened 2022-09-16 with exactly your use cases (parent-child, medical proxy), 30 comments, **closed 2022-12-14 with no resolution posted after 2022-09-23**. The WG consensus visible in-thread: *"cryptographic delegation for the purposes of authz (zcaps, gnap, etc.) are out of scope for VCs… the dangers of misuse are too great."*
- **ZCAP-LD** is `specStatus: "CG-DRAFT"` and its own text says it *"is currently being drafted to be considered as a work item"* — **not a W3C Recommendation, not even an adopted CCG work item.** The string `audit` appears **0 times**. Its abuse section's answer: *"Alice… is able to analyze a log of past invocations… and Alice revokes that capability."* A named administrator reads a log.
- The DID Methods registry (W3C Group Note, **2026-09-02**) lists **~600 methods** and explicitly *"does not act as an endorsement."*

**Academic footprint:** "On Guardianship in Self-Sovereign Identity" (2019) — **3 citations**. "SSI and Guardianship in Practice" (EJLT 13(3), 2022) — **0 citations**, and the abstract concedes *"the gap between the practical (legal) and technical perspective."* On Hacker News, Sovrin's peak score across its entire history is **4 points**.

