## C. CUSTODIAL → SELF-CUSTODIAL

Covered in the headline. Additional mechanism detail worth carrying:

**did:plc**: the DID is self-certifying, but `rotationKeys` are *"an internal detail of PLC… stored in the operation log"* — **not in the DID document**. A 72-hour window lets a higher-priority key *"'rewrite' history, clobbering any operations… signed by a lower-authority rotation key."* So even a user who adds their own key gets index ≥1 and can be clobbered by the host within 72h. Christine Lemmer-Webber called it in January 2024: *"Bluesky controls most users' keys anyway, so can control their identity future regardless."*

**did:web** trades a company for a registrar: the spec concedes it *"does not provide a mechanism for migration or recovering from loss of control of the domain name."*

**The PLC directory handoff is announced, not shipped.** 2025-09-19, https://atproto.com/blog/plc-directory-org: an independent **Swiss Association** — *"Initial board members and other logistical details are still being finalized."* No launch date, no funding. `plc.directory` remains the single write endpoint. The Feb-2026 read-replica work federates *reads* only.

**Permanent cost of the atproto exit:** *"The full history of DID operations and updates, including timestamps, is permanently publicly accessible… any PII encoded in `alsoKnownAs` URIs will be publicly visible even after DID deactivation, and cannot be redacted."*

**Argent is the one design where guardian authority is genuinely fenced**: guardians alone can never move funds or transfer ownership; recovery takes ⌈n/2⌉ guardians + **48h**, cancellable single-handedly by the owner; guardian changes take 36h to confirm. Compare Farcaster (recovery address alone, instantly) and Bluesky (host alone, instantly). Note: `argent.xyz` now 301s to `ready.co` and the contracts repo has been idle since 2024-07-29; formal sunset **NOT REACHED**.

**ERC-4337 (Final)** standardizes `UserOperation` and `EntryPoint` and explicitly **does not** standardize recovery or key rotation — *"the `signature` field usage is not defined by the protocol."* Every escape hatch is bespoke; none compose.

**Matrix, as a control case for "leave with your identity" at protocol scale:** the FAQ concedes *"Currently, no. We are looking at options for decentralising or migrating user accounts."* Five MSCs have tried — **#1228 open since 2018-05-10** (8+ years), #2787 "Portable Identities" open since 2020-09-23, #4080, #4243, #4348 — **all open, all labeled `needs-implementation`, none merged.** MSC2787 proposes exactly the two-layer model your design implies (User Permanent Key + User Delegated Key) and lists among its unsolved TODOs: *"How to adequately disconnect UDKs from UPKs as a part of a data removal request for GDPR compliance."*

**Nostr's one delegation primitive, NIP-26, was deprecated 2025-04-29** and now carries the warning *"unrecommended: adds unnecessary burden for little gain."*

---

## D. ENTERPRISE — data copies only, never the identity

The full table is in the headline. The three structural points:

1. **The asymmetry is architectural.** Every vendor shipped a documented, API-exposed, **consent-free ingestion path** and no egress path. Microsoft ships `forceTakeover: true`; there is no `forceRelease`. Apple's Domain Capture: *"Turning on Domain Capture can't be undone"*, users get **30 days**, notifications at day 1/21/27, and the default on no action is a rename to `elana-townshipschools.org@temporary.appleaccount.com` (all verified verbatim). Google's transfer tool: *"A user cannot decline a request; however the user can ignore it"* — and ignoring means *"they will need to rename their personal account."* Entra external admin takeover moves **users, subscriptions and licenses** on the strength of a DNS TXT record, then **deletes the source directory 10 days later**, with no consent step anywhere in the flow.

2. **What survives into the person's hands is an account they already held — and it survives only by surrendering its name.** Apple → `@temporary.appleaccount.com`. Microsoft → `user@fourthcoffeexyz.onmicrosoft.com`. Google → *"rename their personal account."* **The identifier, not the account, is what the org actually takes.**

3. **The orphan is a documented feature, not an implementation bug.** RFC 7644 §3.6: providers *"MAY choose not to permanently delete the resource but MUST return a 404."* RFC 7643 `active`: *"The definitive meaning of this attribute is **determined by the service provider**."* The only standardized deactivation signal is normatively defined as having no normative meaning. Microsoft's own **"Known limitations"**: *"When a user or group is unassigned from an app… a disable request is sent. At that point, the service doesn't manage the user and a delete request isn't sent when the user is soft-deleted from the directory."* The common admin gesture permanently disarms the eventual DELETE. Plus: a provisioning job in quarantine >4 weeks is **disabled**, so an expired credential silently takes an org's whole deprovisioning pipeline offline.

Apple's six-year tombstone is worth noting for any namespace design: a deleted personal Apple Account *"can't be recreated nor can it be used as a Managed Apple Account **for six years**."*

**Published orphan rates: NOT REACHED.** HN, GitHub, and Crossref all empty. Vendor surveys were deliberately not used — the Microsoft "Known limitations" text is a strictly better artifact: first-party, dated, a *mechanism*, and it needs no sample.

---

## E. ROOTLESSNESS — where the human gets re-attached anyway

### The one place a rootless chain is designed

Miller's *Robust Composition* is genuinely rootless **in the model**. §9.2.5: *"only connectivity begets connectivity—all access must derive from previous access."* §9.2.1 postulates the root away: *"there is always a first instant of time."* But every time the thesis names the origin it is a person (§11.3 *"the owner of the system"*; §7.4 *"the human operator… read the URI from his screen over the phone"*). Measured: `"ambient authority"`, `"accountability"`, `"provenance"` and `"non-repudiation"` each appear **zero times** in the thesis. Note also `erights.org` is **unreachable** (connection refused, both ports) as of today; the thesis survives on Agoric's mirror.

**The ocap authors published the gap themselves.** Miller/Donnelley/Karp, HotSec'07: *"although many historical criticisms of ocaps have since been refuted, **a remaining unrefuted criticism is that they cannot record who to blame for which action**. **This lack has led some to forego the benefits of ocaps**."* Their answer — Horton, a sealer/unsealer "Who" object that is explicitly *not* a person — **ships nowhere**: zero hits in `Agoric/agoric-sdk`, `ocapn/ocapn`, `endojs/endo`, and the download host is dead.

**OCapN**, the closest thing in the world to your design, is **pre-specification**, and grep across its three spec files finds **zero occurrences of human, person, audit, accountability, provenance, owner, or revocation.** It has no answer because it has no concept.

### The standards say a chain's root has no authority

**RFC 8693** (OAuth Token Exchange) is the one in-force IETF standard modeling a delegation chain via nested `act` claims — *"The outermost `act` claim represents the current actor while nested `act` claims represent prior actors."* And then: *"For the purpose of applying access control policy, the consumer of a token **MUST only consider** the token's top-level claims and the party identified as the **current actor**… **Prior actors identified by any nested `act` claims are informational only and are not to be considered in access control decisions**."*

**The chain's history is decorative.** That is exactly what your design wants (nothing climbs to a human) and exactly the hazard: nothing about where an actor came from constrains what it may do.

**RFC 9068** permits a subject with no human — for client-credentials tokens *"the value of 'sub' SHOULD correspond to an identifier the authorization server uses to indicate the client application"* — while warning that the AS must prevent resource servers from confusing a person-token with a program-token. **This is the Sovrin "Wallet Take-Over" warning restated in OAuth terms.** Two unrelated traditions arrived at the same finding independently: *a verifier facing an unrooted identity cannot tell what kind of thing it is dealing with.*

### The re-attachment points, ranked by hardness

1. **Creation-time platform requirement.** Entra Agent ID (doc updated **2026-09-03**, quoted verbatim): a service principal **may be an owner** — *"Service principals as owners enable automated management of agent identities"* — but *"**A sponsor is required when creating an agent identity or agent blueprint**"*, sponsors may be *"Users (including guest users) and groups"* only, and *"**For app-only create requests, the creating service must set one or more users or supported groups as the sponsor.**"* That is precisely agent-creates-agent with no interactive human, and Microsoft refuses the create.
2. **Payment / billing.** AWS Customer Agreement §2.1 (2026-08-14): *"you must have an AWS account associated with a valid email address and a valid form of payment"* and *"you are responsible for all activities that occur under your account."* `organizations:CreateAccount` is machine-callable, but the owner email *"becomes the user name credential for the root user"*, contact details are copied down, and everything bills to one payer account. GCP: the payments profile stores *"the name, address, and tax ID (when required legally) of who is responsible."*
3. **Licensing.** Microsoft Agent 365 *"requires a Microsoft Agent 365 license for each user."* Agents are billed per seat.
4. **ToS.** Anthropic Commercial Terms: *"Customer is responsible for all activity under its account."*
5. **Statutory fallback — the sharpest legal finding.** EU AMLR (Regulation 2024/1624), applying **10 July 2027**, **Art. 22(2)**: *"Where, after having exhausted all possible means of identification, **no natural persons are identified as beneficial owners**… obliged entities shall record that no beneficial owner was identified and **identify all the natural persons holding the positions of senior managing officials** in the legal entity and shall verify their identity."* Art. 63(4) mirrors it at the register. Recital (125) concedes the fiction: *"Although they are identified in those situations, the senior managing officials **are not the beneficial owners**."* Recital (126) forecloses the workaround: *"Difficulties in obtaining the information should not be a valid reason to avoid the identification effort."*
   **A structure engineered so no chain climbs to a natural person does not produce "no answer." It produces a substituted answer — a named human by office, plus a justification the authorities may attack.**
   *(Jurisdictional asymmetry, measured: the US switched its equivalent OFF. FinCEN's interim rule of 2025-03-26, finalized 2026-08-11 effective 2026-08-14, narrows "reporting company" to foreign-formed entities only; **all US-formed entities and US persons are exempt.**)*
6. **Regulatory role typing.** GDPR Art. 4(7): controller is *"the natural or legal person, public authority, agency or other body."* AI Act (2024/1689) Art. 3: the *system* is *"machine-based… with varying levels of autonomy"*, but provider, deployer, importer, distributor, authorised representative — all seven roles collapsed into "operator" — are each typed **"a natural or legal person."** The autonomy sits in Art. 3(1); the liability sits in Art. 3(3)–(8), and it is a person. Electronic personhood was proposed (EP resolution 2017-02-16 §59(f)) and **rejected** (2018 open letter); what was enacted instead types every accountable role as a person.
7. **Court order — and this is where the cost is measured.** Three cases, three different attachment mechanics:
   - **Ooki DAO.** CFTC characterized it as *"an unincorporated association of which Bean and Kistner were actively participating members."* Default judgment 2023-06-09 (Judge Orrick): DAO is a "person" under the CEA, $643,542 penalty, and orders binding *third-party web-hosting and domain-name registrars* to remove the site. Commissioner Mersinger's dissent (2022-09-22) names the cost: liability attached to token holders *"based solely on their status as members"*, and *"token holder A has now become a member of the unincorporated association and (possibly unknowingly) assumed personal liability… whereas token holder B, by the happenstance of not voting, has not."* **The act of participating, performed inside the system, retroactively created the human root.**
   - **Roman Storm** (2025-08-06): convicted on 1 of 3 counts, jury deadlocked on the rest. Prosecution theory: he *"had the ability to modify Tornado Cash's code to prevent illicit use… but chose not to."* **Residual capability is ownership.** Whoever can still change it is the owner, whatever the design says.
   - **Van Loon v. Treasury**, 5th Cir. No. 23-50669, **2024-11-26**: Tornado Cash's immutable contracts are *"unownable, uncontrollable, and unchangeable—**even by its creators**"* and therefore *"not the 'property' of a foreign national or entity"* and not blockable under IEEPA. OFAC delisted 2025-03-21. **Genuine ownerlessness is legally real — and it bought immunity by forfeiting every protection ownership confers.** The contracts can never be updated, revoked, or repaired. And the case only reached a court because six named natural persons had standing to bring it.

---

## FLAG: failures the designer would likely not have priced

**1. The manager relation is the abuse vector, and the tooling built for parent→child is the most prevalent instance of it.** Havron et al., USENIX Security '19 (N=44 clients, 46 consultations, 105 devices): **23/44 (52%)** had identified security risks. Of 79 apps flagged as problematic, **61 were dual-use, "with 'find my phone' and child monitoring apps the most prevalent categories."** Four clients remained on a shared family plan the abuser paid for; twelve had unrecognized "trusted" devices. Every parental-control affordance is, empirically, also a surveillance affordance.

**2. The law gives the manager a delete right and the managed party none.** COPPA §312.6 vests every right in the **parent** — review, refuse further collection, *"direct the operator to delete the child's personal information."* The child has no counterpart right anywhere in 16 CFR Part 312, and **the FTC's own COPPA compliance FAQ contains no guidance whatsoever on the age-13 transition.** §312.10 then *requires* deletion once the collection purpose ends. Under GDPR the only right the grown child gets over their childhood-managed data is Recital 65 — a right to **erase** it (*"The data subject should be able to exercise that right notwithstanding the fact that he or she is no longer a child"*), not to inherit it. Art. 20 portability is a right to a **copy** in a machine-readable format, qualified *"where technically feasible"*, and §20(4) says it *"shall not adversely affect the rights and freedoms of others"* — which includes the manager persona. **Both legal regimes point at deletion. Neither contemplates handover.** ICO Children's Code: **NOT REACHED** (403).

**3. Rootlessness is achievable for ownership and impossible for initiation.** Safe permits contract-owns-contract at arbitrary depth — owners *"can be EOAs, other smart contract accounts, or even a passkey."* But ethereum.org states the physics: *"Contract accounts… **can only send messages in response to receiving a transaction**."* EIP-7702 (Final) preserves this — the transaction is still EOA-originated and EOA-signed. A fully rootless ownership graph is **inert**. Something must hold a key and press go, and that something is where every investigation will start.

**4. The deepest technical cost falls on the *managed* party, not the manager.** `did:key` — the only DID method that is genuinely registry-free and owner-of-record-free, i.e. the exact shape a rootless persona needs — states: *"This DID Method does not support updating the DID Document"*; *"does not support deactivating"*; *"does not support key rotation because the identifier is derived directly from the public key material itself"*; and *"using a `did:key` for interactions that last weeks to months is **strongly discouraged** unless it is coupled with a hardware security module."* CCG draft v0.9, never a Recommendation. **A rootless identity that cannot rotate, recover, or be deactivated is not a durable home for a person's life; it is an ephemeral handle with a permanent lifespan.**

**5. The transition, not the relation, is where the value is destroyed.** Every system measured makes the *relation* cheap and the *exit* expensive: Apple confiscates the Apple Cash balance upward and strands in-app purchases inside a parent-bought app; Google's deletion of the family *group* destroys the restore path for the *account*; atproto's exit writes an unerasable public record of every handle you ever had; Sovrin's exit is a re-issuance that depends on issuers still existing (Kiva's did not, Sovrin's own foundation did not). **A design that models the manager→managed relation carefully and the transition loosely will lose exactly what the managed party was promised.**

**6. A design that names no owner should expect one to be assigned.** Ooki attached at *who voted*. Storm attached at *who could still change it*. AMLR Art. 22(2) attaches at *whoever holds a senior managing office*. Entra attaches at *create time, or the create fails*. AWS attaches at *the email that became the root user*. The only case where nothing attached — Van Loon — succeeded because the object was provably beyond anyone's reach, including its authors', forever.

---

## Tooling notes for the next pass

- **OpenAlex budget exhausted mid-session** (HTTP 429, *"Resets at midnight UTC"*). Use Crossref/arXiv until then.
- **Google support pages now CAPTCHA our egress** (`www.google.com/sorry/`) after sustained fetching; they worked earlier in the session.
- Newly confirmed blocked: `erights.org` (connection refused), `fatf-gafi.org` (403), `federalregister.gov` / `ecfr.gov` (redirect to `unblock.federalregister.gov`) — **use `law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/...` instead, which works**; `ico.org.uk` (403); `reddit.com` (all forms); `discussions.apple.com` via curl but **not** via WebFetch.
- Newly confirmed working: `support.apple.com/kb/index?page=search&q=…&locale=en_US` is server-rendered and is the only reliable way to find Apple article IDs; `developer.apple.com/tutorials/data/documentation/<lower>.json` returns full content where the SPA returns only `<title>`; `learn.microsoft.com` returns full markdown with front-matter including `updated_at`; Google Drive `uc?export=download&id=…` retrieves the unpublished Sovrin guardianship documents.
